Installing Xymon under systemd
==============================

The build can install a systemd service unit for xymonlaunch, generated from
tools/xymonlaunch.service.DIST with the paths the tree was configured with. It
is off unless you ask for it.

Install variables
-----------------

INSTALLSYSTEMDDIR
    Where the unit file is installed, e.g. /usr/lib/systemd/system (packages)
    or /etc/systemd/system (a local install). Set it before or at configure
    time, or pass it on the "make install" command line.

    Unset -- the default -- install-systemd does nothing and no unit is
    installed. That is what a source install on a host without systemd wants;
    tools/xymonlaunch.service.DIST is then only a sample.

INSTALLENVDIR
    Where the generated unit reads its EnvironmentFile, and where the
    xymonlaunch options file (tools/xymonlaunch.default) belongs:
    /etc/sysconfig on Red Hat and SUSE, /etc/default on Debian, and by default
    the tree's own etc/, which is where a source install reads it from.

What is and is not installed
----------------------------

install-systemd installs one file: the generated xymonlaunch.service. The
other files here are samples and are NOT installed -- copy the ones your
layout needs into place yourself, or let your packaging do it:

    xymonlaunch.default          xymonlaunch options (goes to INSTALLENVDIR)
    xymonlaunch.service.preset   an enablement preset for systemctl preset
    xymon-tmpfiles.conf          a tmpfiles.d fragment for the runtime dir

Enabling the unit
-----------------

The build installs the unit file only; it does not enable or start it.
Whether xymonlaunch runs at boot is a local or distribution policy decision --
`systemctl enable xymonlaunch`, a shipped preset, or a packaging scriptlet.
